    "result_text": "The complete test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`). There's a single test class (`AcceptanceTest`); the other files are the base/helper classes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag following the exact pattern already established for the sibling `bulkSignupWarning` flag.\n\n**The logic** (`ClinicServiceImpl.createOwner`): after the existing hard-capacity check (which throws at 50), the owner is flagged when the city already holds at least 40 owners. Since counts of 50+ are already rejected above, `ownersInCity >= CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD` cleanly captures the 40\u201349 range without duplicating the bound:\n\n```java\nowner.setCapacityWarning(ownersInCity >= CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD);\n```\n\nwith a new `static final int CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40;` constant documented alongside `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY`. The existing hard rejection at 50 is untouched.\n\n**Supporting changes**, each mirroring how `bulkSignupWarning` is wired end-to-end:\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with getter/setter, so it persists and survives the create\u2192read round-trip the test exercises.\n- The four DB schemas (`h2`, `hsqldb`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning` column.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` property so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps it by name (no mapper change needed).\n\nThe reused count (`ownersInCity`) and the existing warning/mapping infrastructure meant no logic was duplicated and no class grew a new responsibility \u2014 the service simply sets one more derived flag where it already sets the others.",
